  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The air in the lighthouse keeper’s cottage had grown thick with the scent of damp wool and stale tea. Elias sat by the window, his fingers tracing the condensation that beaded on the cold glass, watching the horizon where the sky had turned a bruised purple. For three days, the barometer had been dropping steadily, a silent warning that the sea was stirring with a fury it rarely displayed. He had checked the oil reserves, wound the great clockwork mechanism that turned the lens, and ensured the signal lamp was ready, but the true test was yet to come. Outside, the wind began to howl, a low, mournful sound that seemed to vibrate through the very stones of the tower. The waves, usually a rhythmic lullaby, now crashed against the base of the lighthouse with violent, irregular thuds, sending plumes of white foam high into the air. Elias remembered his father’s words, spoken years ago in this very spot: \"The sea does not forgive, Elias. It only waits.\" He had always dismissed it as old-man superstition, but tonight, the weight of those words pressed heavily on his chest. He stood up, his joints creaking in protest, and moved to the heavy oak door. The wind battered against it, trying to force its way in, but the door held firm. He took a deep breath, the cold air biting at his lungs, and reached for the lantern. The light flickered, casting long, dancing shadows across the room. He knew what he had to do. He had to go out there, into the storm, to check the mooring lines of the supply boat that had been stuck in the harbor for weeks. If the lines snapped, the boat would be dragged out to sea, and the supplies they needed to survive the winter would be lost. He pulled on his oilskins, the heavy fabric rustling as he moved, and grabbed his coat. The storm was no longer a distant threat; it was here, and it was hungry."
}

{
