  "text": "The silence of the previous evening had been deceptive, a calm that Elias had learned to distrust over his forty years of service. He had spent the afternoon repairing the frayed rope on the main ladder, his hands calloused and steady despite the tremor that had begun to creep into his left arm. The village below, usually a cluster of warm lights and distant laughter, had gone dark early, the residents retreating into their homes as the first grey clouds rolled in from the west. He had watched them from the gallery, noting the way the gulls had vanished, leaving the sky empty and unnervingly still. There was a specific quality to the air that day, a metallic tang that coated the back of his throat, signaling a change in pressure that no instrument could fully capture. He had tried to call the harbor master, but the line was dead, a static hiss that offered no comfort. The isolation of the lighthouse, usually a source of peace, now felt like a cage. He had prepared a simple meal of bread and cheese, eating it slowly while listening to the subtle shifts in the wind’s direction. It had started as a whisper, a gentle nudge against the tower’s stone skin, but by dusk, it had grown into a persistent drone. The sea, once a flat expanse of silver, had begun to ripple, the waves growing taller and more aggressive. Elias had checked the logs, noting the date and the weather conditions with mechanical precision, a ritual that grounded him in the present moment. He had told himself he was ready, that he had faced worse storms, but a nagging doubt lingered in the back of his mind. The supply boat, the *Perseverance*, sat in the harbor, its hull groaning against the pilings. It was their lifeline, the only connection to the mainland, and if it was lost, they would be truly alone. He had spent the last hour staring at the horizon, waiting for the first sign of the storm’s true nature, knowing that when it arrived, there would be no time for hesitation, only action."
