  "text": "The night had been a long, unbroken stretch of silence, broken only by the rhythmic, mechanical hum of the lighthouse beam sweeping across the dark water. Elias had spent the hours in the lamp room, his eyes fixed on the horizon, watching for any sign of the storm that the barometer had promised. The needle had dropped steadily since sunset, a slow, terrifying descent that mirrored the growing pressure in his chest. He had tried to sleep, but the dreams were restless, filled with the sound of crashing waves and the cries of men lost to the sea. He remembered his father, who had kept this light for forty years before the heart attack took him. His father had always said that the sea was a living thing, a beast that slept and woke at its own discretion. Elias had dismissed it as superstition, a way for old men to make sense of the chaos. But now, as he sat in the dim glow of the control panel, he felt the truth of those words. The air outside was thick, heavy with the scent of ozone and salt. He could feel it in his bones, a deep, resonant vibration that seemed to come from the earth itself. He had checked the radio, but there was only static, a white noise that hissed and crackled, offering no comfort, no information. The village below was dark, the windows of the cottages reflecting the faint, pulsing light of the lighthouse. He wondered if anyone else was awake, if anyone else felt the weight of the coming storm. He thought of the fishermen, their boats tied up in the harbor, vulnerable to the fury that was building. He thought of the children, sleeping in their beds, unaware of the danger that loomed over them. He felt a surge of protectiveness, a fierce, burning need to keep them safe. He knew he couldn't stop the storm, but he could prepare for it. He could ensure that the light stayed on, that the beacon remained a guide in the darkness. He stood up, his legs stiff from the long hours of sitting, and began to make his way down the spiral staircase, his mind already turning to the tasks that awaited him in the morning."
