  "text": "The safehouse was a nondescript brick building wedged between a shuttered laundromat and a 24-hour diner, its windows dark and uninviting. Elias checked his watch, the digital display glowing a faint, sickly green in the gloom of the alleyway. 02:14. He had four minutes before the patrol drones swept the sector, their red scanning beams cutting through the fog like surgical lasers. He pressed his back against the cold, damp wall, his breath coming in shallow, controlled bursts. The drive was tucked securely inside his boot, a hidden pocket he had sewn into the lining hours ago. It felt heavier than it should, a physical manifestation of the weight he carried. He closed his eyes, trying to block out the distant wail of sirens and the rhythmic thumping of the rain against the pavement. His mind drifted back to the briefing, the sterile white room, the holographic map of the server farm. The Director’s voice had been calm, almost soothing, as he outlined the objective. 'Retrieve the data, Elias. Do not engage. Do not leave a trace.' But the reality on the ground had been anything but clean. The guards had been augmented, their reflexes sharpened by neural implants, making them terrifyingly fast. Elias had barely survived the initial breach, relying on instinct and the sheer desperation of a man with nowhere else to go. He opened his eyes, scanning the street. A shadow moved in the distance, a figure hunched against the wind. He tensed, his hand drifting instinctively to the empty holster at his hip. Was it a tracker? A stray? Or just another soul lost in the city’s endless night? He waited, the seconds stretching into eternity, the rain soaking through his jacket, chilling him to the bone. The figure stopped, turning slightly, and Elias held his breath, his heart hammering against his ribs like a trapped bird. He knew he couldn’t stay here. The safehouse was close, but the danger was closer. He had to move, had to keep moving, or he would never see the sun again."
