  "text": "The extraction point had been a disaster. Elias remembered the chaos of the server farm, the way the alarms had screamed in a frequency that vibrated in his teeth. He had been moving through the sub-levels, the air thick with the smell of ozone and burnt circuitry, when the first guard had appeared. It wasn’t a human, not really. The neural implants had stripped away the hesitation, the fear, leaving only a cold, efficient machine of muscle and metal. Elias had fired twice, both shots finding their mark, but the creature didn’t fall. It simply adjusted, its eyes glowing with a predatory red light, and lunged. The fight had been brutal, a tangle of limbs and sparks in the dark. He had managed to sever the connection to the mainframe, the drive clutched in his hand, but the cost had been high. His left arm was a mess of bruised tissue and torn skin, the pain a dull, throbbing constant that he had learned to ignore. He had barely escaped the facility, slipping through a maintenance tunnel that smelled of rust and stale water. The city outside was a labyrinth of neon and shadow, the rain falling in sheets that blurred the world into a smear of color. He had moved quickly, avoiding the main thoroughfares, sticking to the alleys and the underpasses where the surveillance cameras had blind spots. But the drones were persistent, their red beams sweeping the streets with mechanical precision. He had lost them once, hiding in a dumpster behind a noodle shop, the stench of rotting food making him gag. But they had found him again, the sound of their rotors growing louder, closer. He had run, his lungs burning, his legs feeling like lead. The safehouse was his only hope, a place where the Director’s people could wipe his memory and send him back to the shadows. But he had to get there before the drones did. The alleyway was narrow, the walls closing in on him, the rain drumming against the pavement like a countdown. He checked his watch again, the green light reflect