  "text": "The extraction point was supposed to be simple. A quick in-and-out, grab the drive, and vanish into the night. But the city of Neo-Veridia had other plans. Elias remembered the moment the alarm klaxons first wailed, a shrill, piercing sound that cut through the sterile silence of the server room. Kael had been the first to react, his tactical visor flickering with red warnings as he scanned the perimeter. \"They’re here,\" Kael had whispered, his voice tight with controlled panic. \"Too many of them. We’re compromised.\" Jinx, usually the calmest of the trio, had dropped to one knee, her fingers flying across her wrist-mounted interface, trying to jam the local security feeds. \"I can buy us thirty seconds,\" she had said, her eyes wide and unblinking. \"Maybe forty if I overload the main breaker. But after that, it’s on us.\" Elias had nodded, the heavy, encrypted drive already secured in his inner pocket. It felt like a lead weight, a physical manifestation of the danger they were in. They had moved fast, sprinting through the labyrinthine corridors of the corporate tower, dodging the first wave of automated turrets that burst from the walls with a hiss of steam. The air was thick with the smell of ozone and burnt plastic. As they reached the service elevator, the doors began to close, but a heavy, metallic clang stopped them. A drone, sleek and black, hovered in the doorway, its single red eye locking onto them. \"Go!\" Kael had shouted, firing his pulse rifle. The shot missed, sparking against the metal frame. Jinx had slammed her hand onto the emergency release, and the doors slid open just enough for them to squeeze through. The elevator plummeted, the G-force pressing them against the floor. When the doors opened, they were not in the lobby, but in a maintenance shaft, far below street level. The rain was already audible, a distant roar that grew louder as they climbed the rusted ladder. They emerged into the alleyway, soaked and gasping, only to find the exit blocked by a wall of armed guards. The mission had gone sideways, and now, they were alone."
