  "text": "The rain lashed against the reinforced glass of the observation deck, blurring the neon skyline of Neo-Veridia into a smear of electric blue and sickly yellow. Elias stood with his back to the window, watching the reflection of the city flicker in the dark. He had spent three days in this sterile, high-rise sanctuary, waiting for the right moment, the right window of opportunity. The Helios Corporation’s headquarters loomed below, a monolith of steel and secrets that had swallowed thousands whole. He checked his watch, the digital display glowing a faint, reassuring green. 02:00 AM. The shift change was in ten minutes. It was the only time the security grid would cycle, creating a brief, microscopic gap in the surveillance net. Beside him, Kael paced, his boots scuffing against the polished floor. The young man’s anxiety was a physical presence, a static charge that made the hair on Elias’s arms stand up. \"Are you sure about this?\" Kael asked, his voice cracking under the weight of the impending danger. \"If we’re wrong, if the encryption is tighter than we think...\" Elias cut him off, his voice low and steady. \"We are not wrong. Mara has the keys. We just need to be in the right place at the right time.\" He turned, his eyes hard, devoid of the doubt that plagued his companion. \"We go down the service shaft. It’s dirty, it’s dangerous, but it’s invisible to the drones. We meet Mara in the sub-basement. She handles the terminal. We handle the exit.\" Kael swallowed hard, nodding slowly. He reached into his jacket, pulling out a small, battered device. \"I’ve got the signal boosters ready. If we get stuck, I can jam the local comms for thirty seconds. It’s not much, but it’s something.\" Elias took the device, weighing it in his palm. It felt light, insignificant, yet it held the power to save or doom them all. \"Keep it close,\" Elias said, his gaze drifting back to the window, to the dark, humming tower below. \"And stay quiet. The moment we step into that shaft, there is no turning back. We are ghosts now. And ghosts do not make noise.\" The rain intensified, drumming a frantic rhythm against the glass, mirroring the pounding in Elias’s chest. He took a deep breath, the air cold and sterile, and prepared to descend into the dark."
}

