  "text": "The safehouse smelled of stale coffee and old fear. Elias sat on the edge of the narrow bed, staring at the rain-streaked window, while Kael paced the small room like a caged animal. The city outside was a blur of gray and neon, the storm battering the glass with a rhythmic, violent persistence. Kael stopped, turning to face him, his eyes dark and unreadable. \"You’re sure about this?\" he asked, his voice low, barely a whisper over the thunder. Elias didn’t look up. \"I’m sure about the data. I’m not sure about you.\" The silence that followed was heavy, thick with unspoken accusations and shared trauma. Kael let out a dry, humorless laugh. \"Five years, Elias. We’ve burned bridges, broken laws, and buried friends. If I was going to sell you out, I would have done it years ago. This is the one job that matters. The one that ends it all.\" Elias finally met his gaze, searching for a flicker of deceit, but found only exhaustion and a desperate, burning hope. He remembered the faces of the citizens in the lower sectors, their eyes hollowed out by the constant surveillance, the lack of privacy, the crushing weight of the Council’s control. He thought of Mara, who had lost her brother to the same system, her grief a constant, aching companion. \"If you lie to me, Kael, I won’t just kill you. I’ll make sure your name is forgotten. I’ll erase you from history.\" Kael nodded slowly, a grim smile touching his lips. \"Fair enough. The shift change is in two hours. The security protocols reset for a brief window. It’s our only chance. Get some sleep, Elias. You’ll need it. The night is going to be long, and the Council won’t be waiting for us to make a mistake.\" Elias stood up, his muscles tense, his mind already racing through the plan. He checked his gear, the server rack key cold against his palm. The rain continued to fall, washing the city clean, but it couldn’t wash away the darkness that was coming. He was ready. He had to be."
