  "text": "The rain had been falling for three days, a relentless, gray curtain that turned the streets of Aethelgard into a mirror of the city’s decaying grandeur. Elias sat in his cramped apartment, the walls thin enough to hear the muffled arguments of his neighbors, the clatter of the mag-lev trains overhead, and the distant, rhythmic thrum of the geothermal plants that powered their world. He was a junior analyst, a cog in the vast machine of the Council’s infrastructure, tasked with monitoring the deep-core sensors that kept the city’s foundations stable. It was tedious work, repetitive and soul-crushing, but it paid the rent and kept the lights on. He had long since stopped asking questions, stopped wondering why the Council kept the lower sectors sealed off, why the data from the deepest levels was always redacted, why the hum that vibrated through the floorboards at night seemed to grow louder with each passing month. He had made his peace with the silence, with the comfortable ignorance that kept him safe. But tonight, the silence felt different. It was heavy, oppressive, like the air before a storm. He stared at the ceiling, the water stain in the corner looking like a map of a place he had never seen. He thought of his mother, who had disappeared into the lower sectors five years ago, her last words a whisper about something waking up, something ancient and hungry. He had dismissed it as madness, a symptom of the isolation and the dark. But now, as the rain hammered against the window, he felt a strange pull in his chest, a resonance that matched the hum in the walls. It was subtle, almost imperceptible, but it was there, a thread connecting him to something vast and unknown beneath the city. He knew he should ignore it, should go to sleep, should wake up to another day of mundane data entry. But the pull was getting stronger, and for the first time in years, he felt a spark of curiosity, a dangerous, forbidden curiosity that threatened to unravel the carefully constructed lie of his life."
