  "text": "The city of Aethelgard did not sleep; it merely dimmed. As the sun dipped below the horizon, the towering spires of the Council’s administrative district flickered to life, their crystalline facades reflecting the perpetual twilight that had become the norm. Below, in the shadowed underbelly of the metropolis, the neon signs buzzed with a sickly, electric hum, advertising synthetic nutrients and memory wipes to the weary populace. Elias watched the rain begin to fall from the window of his small, rented room, the droplets tracing erratic paths down the glass, mirroring the chaotic thoughts swirling in his mind. He had spent the day in the sterile, white-walled offices of the Infrastructure Division, staring at screens filled with cascading data streams that meant nothing to him. The numbers were cold, impersonal, and utterly devoid of the human element he craved. His supervisor, a man with eyes like polished steel, had reminded him that efficiency was the only virtue that mattered in Aethelgard. Emotions were inefficiencies, and inefficiencies were pruned. Elias had nodded, his face a mask of professional detachment, while inside, a quiet rebellion simmered. He thought of the stories his mother used to tell, tales of a time before the Council, before the geothermal plants, before the sky turned gray. She had spoken of stars, of wind that smelled of pine, of a world that was not built on the backs of the forgotten. He had dismissed them as fairy tales, necessary myths to keep the children from asking too many questions. But lately, the myths felt less like stories and more like memories, fragments of a truth he had been conditioned to forget. The rain intensified, drumming against the windowpane, a rhythmic pulse that seemed to sync with his own heartbeat. He felt a sudden, sharp pain in his chest, a phantom ache that he could not explain. It was as if something deep within him was stirring, waking from a long, enforced slumber. He looked at his hands, pale and trembling, and wondered if he was losing his mind, or if he was finally beginning to see."
