  "text": "The night had been a blur of static and silence, the kind of heavy quiet that pressed against the eardrums like deep water. Elias lay in his narrow bunk, the synthetic fabric of the sheet cool against his skin, staring at the ceiling where a single, dim light flickered with a rhythmic, almost mocking pulse. He had tried to sleep, but the city’s ambient noise, usually a comforting white noise, had turned into a cacophony of whispers in his mind. He remembered the last few days, the way the air in the ventilation shafts had tasted metallic, the way the nutrient paste had lacked its usual bland consistency, carrying a faint, unidentifiable bitterness that made his stomach churn. He had reported it, of course, following protocol, but the response had been a curt dismissal from the automated system, a red light blinking on his terminal before the issue was marked as resolved. It was not resolved. It was ignored. He thought of his neighbor, Kael, who had stopped coming to the communal dining hall three days ago. No one spoke of it. No one asked. The absence was simply filled by the next person in line, a seamless replacement in the machine of Aethelgard. Elias closed his eyes, trying to block out the flickering light, but the image of Kael’s empty bunk remained, a stark void in the gray monotony of his life. He wondered if Kael had been taken, or if he had simply ceased to exist, erased from the records as if he had never been. The fear was a cold stone in his chest, growing heavier with each passing minute. He needed to know. He needed to find out what was happening to the people who disappeared, to the ones who were no longer there. He sat up, the springs of the bed creaking in the silence, and reached for his badge, his fingers trembling slightly as he clipped it to his chest. He had to go. He had to see. The dream was waiting for him, a promise of something real, something alive, something that might finally break the spell of the gray."
