  "text": "The hum of the ventilation system had been the only constant for the last forty-eight hours, a low-frequency drone that Elias had long since stopped hearing consciously. It was the sound of Aethelgard breathing, or so the propaganda feeds claimed, a rhythmic pulse that kept the city’s millions of inhabitants synchronized in their daily routines. But lately, the rhythm had stuttered. It was subtle, a micro-pause in the airflow that felt less like a mechanical glitch and more like a held breath. Elias had noticed it first in the Sector 4 corridors, where the lights dimmed for a fraction of a second before snapping back to their sterile, white brilliance. He had told himself it was fatigue, a side effect of the new nutrient formula that left him feeling hollowed out, his thoughts sluggish and prone to drifting into dark, unbidden corners. He had ignored the warning signs, the way the other citizens did, because questioning the system was the first step toward becoming a statistic. He remembered the last time he had seen Kael, standing by the water reclamation unit, his face pale and drawn, eyes wide with a terror that Elias had tried to rationalize away as anxiety. Kael had whispered something about the air tasting like copper, about the silence between the hums growing louder. Elias had nodded, offered a hollow smile, and walked away, the weight of unspoken knowledge pressing against his ribs. Now, as the night deepened, the silence was no longer just an absence of sound; it was a presence, a thick, suffocating entity that wrapped around him. The flickering light above his bunk seemed to mock his inaction, a rhythmic taunt that demanded he acknowledge the truth he had been running from. He could feel the static in the air, a palpable charge that made the hair on his arms stand on end. The city was changing, and he was the only one who could feel the shift in the tectonic plates of their reality. He had to act before the next cycle began, before the system reset and erased the last trace of doubt from his mind."
