  "text": "Elias woke with a gasp, his heart hammering against his ribs like a trapped bird. The dream had been vivid, terrifyingly so: a sky of bruised purple, the spires of Aethelgard crumbling into dust, and Kael’s face, twisted in a silent scream, dissolving into static. He lay frozen in the narrow confines of his bunk, the synthetic sheets damp with sweat, waiting for the adrenaline to recede. The room was dim, lit only by the faint, pulsing blue glow of the status panel on the wall. It read: *Cycle 402. Stability: 98.4%.* The numbers were a lie, he knew, but the comfort of their familiarity was a drug he couldn’t quit. He swung his legs over the side of the bed, his feet touching the cold, metallic floor. The air was still, unnaturally so. Usually, the ventilation system provided a constant, low-level white noise, a lullaby that soothed the collective anxiety of the sector. Today, there was only a faint, high-pitched whine, barely audible, like a tuning fork struck in an empty room. He stood up, his joints stiff from the unnatural sleep, and walked to the small porthole that looked out onto the corridor. The hallway was empty, the sterile white lights humming with a frequency that seemed to vibrate in his teeth. He pressed his hand against the glass, feeling the cold seep into his palm. For a moment, he thought he saw a shadow move in the distance, a flicker of movement that didn’t match the rhythm of the automated maintenance drones. He blinked, and it was gone. Just a trick of the light, or perhaps a trick of his fraying mind. He turned away from the window, his reflection staring back at him, pale and hollow-eyed. The hunger in his stomach was a dull ache, a reminder of the nutrient paste he had consumed hours ago, tasteless and gray. He needed to get to the central hub, to see if the others were feeling the same dissonance, the same creeping sense of wrongness that had taken root in his chest. He grabbed his work tunic, the fabric rough against his skin, and stepped out into the corridor, the door sliding shut behind him with a soft, final hiss."
