  "text": "The corridor lights had begun to flicker an hour ago, a rhythmic pulsing that matched the erratic beating of Elias’s heart. He had ignored the initial warnings from the central AI, dismissing them as minor software glitches, a common occurrence in a vessel this old and this far from any repair dock. But the glitches had grown bolder, more frequent, until the entire starboard wing was bathed in a strobing, disorienting amber glow. He had been in his quarters, trying to sleep, when the floor beneath him had shuddered. It wasn’t the usual vibration of the engines; it was a deep, resonant thrum that seemed to originate from the very bones of the ship. The gravity plating had stuttered, causing his coffee mug to slide off the nightstand and shatter against the metal floor. The sound was sharp, violent, and utterly out of place in the quiet dark. He had grabbed his tool belt, his hands trembling not from fear, but from a strange, electric anticipation. The ship was waking up, or perhaps, it was dying. He couldn’t tell which. The airlock to the maintenance bay was stuck, requiring him to pry it open with a crowbar, the metal screaming in protest. As he stepped through, the temperature dropped sharply. The usual warmth of the engine room was gone, replaced by a biting chill that seeped through his coveralls. The silence was absolute, a vacuum that sucked the sound from his lungs. He walked deeper into the bay, his boots echoing on the grated floor, each step feeling like a trespass. The shadows here were not just absence of light; they were entities, coiling and shifting in the periphery of his vision. He reached the central console, the heart of the ventilation system, and stopped. The readings were impossible. The oxygen levels were normal, the pressure was stable, yet the air felt thick, heavy, as if it were trying to hold its breath. He looked up, meeting the gaze of the security camera, which was no longer recording. The red light was off. The ship was watching him, and it was afraid."
