  "text": "The briefing room was cold, the air recycled so many times it tasted of metal and stale coffee. Elias stood before the holographic map, his reflection ghostly against the swirling blue of the Orion Arm. Around him, the Council members sat in a semicircle, their faces obscured by the shadows of their high-backed chairs. They did not look at him; they looked at the map, at the red zones that pulsed like infected wounds in the fabric of space. \"The *Aethelgard* is not a warship,\" Elias said, his voice steady despite the knot in his stomach. \"It is a scout. If you want to send a fleet, send a fleet. But if you want to know what is out there, you need someone who can turn on a dime and vanish into the dark.\" A long silence followed, broken only by the hum of the ventilation system. Finally, the Chairwoman spoke, her voice devoid of emotion. \"The larger vessels are too expensive to lose. The *Aethelgard* is expendable. Its crew is... selected for their willingness to accept the unknown. You have your orders, Commander. Launch in forty-eight hours.\" Elias nodded, though his mind was already racing. He had served the Council for twenty years, but this mission felt different. It wasn't just exploration; it was a test. A test of the ship, of the crew, and of his own resolve. He looked at the red zones again, wondering what lay beyond them. Was it a new star system? A resource-rich asteroid belt? Or something else? Something that had been hiding in the dark, waiting for someone to find it? He dismissed the thought, focusing instead on the logistics. The *Aethelgard* was old, but it was reliable. Its fusion core was efficient, and its drive array was the most advanced in the fleet. If anyone could survive the journey, it was them. He turned to leave, but paused at the door. \"One more thing,\" he said. \"If we find something... something dangerous... we have the authority to destroy it. No questions asked.\" The Chairwoman’s eyes met his, cold and hard. \"That 