  "text": "The silence in the command center was heavy, broken only by the rhythmic, low-frequency thrum of the station’s life support systems. Vane had not moved from his chair in hours, his fingers resting lightly on the armrests, waiting for the data stream to stabilize. The holographic display flickered, casting erratic shadows across the dimly lit room, but the coordinates remained fixed: the Kaelis Sector. It was a suicide mission, a fact that hung in the air like the metallic taste of ozone. He had spent the last decade running from the ghosts of the Outer Rim, hiding in the shadows of neutral space, but the past had a way of finding you when you least expected it. The message had arrived on an encrypted channel, signed with a cipher that only three people in the galaxy knew. One of them was dead. The other had vanished into the void. That left only one possibility, and it terrified him more than any storm or enemy fleet. He closed his eyes, trying to recall the face of the woman who had sent it, but the memory was fading, blurred by years of synthetic whiskey and regret. The door chime sounded, a soft, polite ping that seemed out of place in the tension-filled room. Vane didn’t open his eyes. He knew who it was. He could hear the hesitation in the footsteps, the slight drag of a boot that had never fully healed from the battle on Titan. He waited, letting the silence stretch until it became unbearable, before finally opening his eyes to meet the gaze of the only person who still believed in the impossible. The room felt smaller now, the walls closing in, as the weight of the decision settled onto his shoulders like a physical burden. He stood up, the joints in his knees protesting the movement, and walked toward the window, where the storm was beginning to rage against the glass."
}

