  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The alert had pierced the quiet of the bridge like a physical blow, shattering the routine of the long haul. Vane had been reviewing the star charts, his mind drifting to the empty ports of the neutral zones, when the red light began to pulse. It was not a standard distress signal, nor a routine traffic update. It was a priority one, encrypted with a key that hadn’t been used in a decade. His heart, usually a steady drum in his chest, skipped a beat, then hammered against his ribs with a frantic rhythm that echoed in his ears. He dismissed the other officers with a sharp wave of his hand, his voice rougher than he intended. \"Clear the deck,\" he ordered, his eyes fixed on the console. The crew, sensing the sudden shift in atmosphere, moved quickly, their boots clanking against the metal grating as they retreated to the lower decks. The door hissed shut, sealing him in with the glowing screen and the weight of the unknown. He typed in the decryption code, his fingers trembling slightly—a rare occurrence for a man who had once held a blaster steady while a plasma bolt grazed his cheek. The screen flickered, then resolved into a single line of text. No coordinates, no demands, just a name. A name that had been erased from every database, every record, every memory he had tried to bury. He stared at it, the letters burning into his retinas, refusing to blink. The air in the room seemed to thicken, making it difficult to draw a full breath. He could feel the eyes of the station’s security drones on the door, waiting for his command, waiting for him to break. He had spent years building a life of anonymity, a quiet existence where the past was a distant, painful echo rather than a present reality. But now, that past was standing at his threshold, demanding an answer. He reached for the comm unit, his hand hovering over the button, paralyzed by the sheer magnitude of what was about to happen. The storm outside was picking up, the wind howling against the hull, a prelude to the chaos that was about to unfold within."
}

